[00:35.84]Conversation One
[00:37.66]W: Hi, Mickey! 
[00:38.69]M: Hi, Suzanne!
[00:40.21]W: Is everything okay£¿You seem kind of depressed.
[00:43.80]M: I got laid off last Friday.
[00:46.11]W: Oh...I'm so sorry to hear that.
[00:49.37]M: They've said that they need to try and save on costs. 
[00:53.08]So they're reducing a bunch of jobs. 
[00:55.89]W: I remember when I got fired from my job as a senior manager in a Web design company, I felt awful for weeks. 
[01:04.21]But you know what£¿
[01:05.37]Getting fired turned out to be the best thing that ever happened to me. 
[01:09.58]It gave me a shock that I really needed. 
[01:12.34]Before that, I was just coasting along in my position day in and day out, never really thinking about my life or my career.
[01:20.82]M: So, getting fired helped you reflect on these things£¿
[01:24.52]W: Absolutely! I started asking myself what I really wanted to do. 
[01:29.45]M: That makes sense. 
[01:31.05]Our daily routines can turn into habits. 
[01:34.36]I've been working at this company for almost 8 years and I have kind of been running on autopilot.
[01:41.83]W: After I was fired, it took me about a year to figure out that opportunities weren't going to come my way, and I had to create my own opportunities. 
[01:51.47]I always had an interest in gardening, so I thought to myself: there must be a lot of people who would like to have a garden but don't have the time to set one up.
[02:01.29]M: So now you go around making custom-designed gardens for homes and offices, right£¿
[02:07.34]W: Yes! I spent almost one year doing odd jobs and trying to figure out what I wanted to do. 
[02:13.79]It was difficult to live without a stable income at that time.
[02:17.61]M: Yeah, right now I have some savings, so I'll be good for about six months. 
[02:23.82]But if I can't find something to do after that, it's going to start getting tough.
[02:29.09]W: Do you have a dream£¿
[02:30.58]M: Well, I have always had the idea of opening a bicycle store. 
[02:35.21]W: Interesting! It just might work!
[02:38.13]M: I never thought I would have a chance to open a bike store. 
[02:41.99]But now I'll think about it! Thanks, Suzanne!
[02:46.17]Questions 1 to 4 are based on the conversation you have just heard.
[02:51.32]1. Why does the man look depressed£¿
[03:08.68]2. Why did the woman consider her being fired as the best thing£¿
[03:26.91]3. What does the woman do now£¿
[03:44.36]4. What will the man probably do next£¿
[04:01.43]Conversation Two
[04:03.40]W: I hate walking into our building in the morning! 
[04:06.48]All the smokers stand around the building's entrance puffing away...I can't take it! 
[04:12.66]Sorry, I'm just a little bit upset.
[04:14.88]M: That's OK. I understand. 
[04:17.73]A lot of people really hate smoke.
[04:20.38]W: I'm glad they banned smoking inside the office, but I wish I didn't have to smell it while walking on the street. 
[04:27.17]It makes me feel awful.
[04:29.03]I simply can't understand why a person would want to smoke.
[04:33.15]M: Well...I used to smoke a lot, but I quit about 20 years ago. 
[04:38.48]You know smoking can definitely damage your health. 
[04:42.24]Most people would quit if they knew the dangers of smoking.
[04:46.11]W: What do you think is the best way to persuade people out of smoking£¿
[04:50.62]M: I think the best way forward is to push education efforts for young people. 
[04:55.91]Most of the time, they choose to smoke because they think it is cool. 
[05:00.40]If we can convince the next generation that smoking isn't ¡°cool¡±, eventually we might be able to eradicate smoking. 
[05:08.90]People's attitudes need to change. 
[05:11.16]W: Right. And also, the prices of cigarettes were increased in order to control smoking. 
[05:17.06]However, the current high prices might not confine most people as it was expected to. 
[05:22.67]You know as cigarettes get more expensive, smokers tend to look for a cheaper option and they will turn to fake ones. 
[05:30.17]I wonder if those would be even worse than the originals! 
[05:33.73]M: You're probably right. 
[05:35.61]But I do think smoking will not be nearly as common a couple decades from now. 
[05:41.01]A newspaper report says that New Zealand hopes to eradicate smoking by 2025.
[05:47.76]W: That's not that long from now! 
[05:49.95]Maybe I should start thinking about moving to New Zealand!
[05:53.65]M: Perhaps you should!
[05:55.91]Questions 5 to 8 are based on the conversation you have just heard.
[06:00.73]5. Why is the woman upset£¿
[06:17.02]6. What do we know about the man£¿
[06:32.74]7. What is the best way to prevent smoking according to the man£¿
[06:51.93]8. What can we learn about smoking in New Zealand in 2025£¿
