[17:46.22]Recording Two
[17:48.22]Today I'm going to introduce some effective skills for taking notes. 
[17:53.78]Before making suggestions I'd like to analyse some factors that can affect note-taking. 
[17:59.86]Of course the first one is your vocabulary. 
[18:03.01]An instructor cannot spell all words in class. 
[18:06.97]So it is necessary for students to master a massive vocabulary, especially for non-native students of the English language. 
[18:15.93]Second, good psychology is also important for note-taking. 
[18:20.98]Some students are very nervous in class and cannot follow the instructor in time although they have a good ability to take notes. 
[18:30.30]Well, what skills can help you improve the ability and the speed of note-taking£¿
[18:35.96]Let's begin with the first one of five steps. 
[18:39.43]As usual, students can get course handouts in advance. 
[18:43.89]Preparing for them will help you know about what the instructor will talk about in class and then help with your note-taking. 
[18:52.01]The second step is to put a date and a title on the first page. 
[18:56.46]This will help you make sure about the lecture topic and the class in which the note is taken. 
[19:02.73]Some students also think this is very important for their studies later. 
[19:07.59]And then I advise you to use a divider which can set aside different parts in one notebook. 
[19:14.37]For example, draw a line in the middle of the notebook and then write main points on the left and some extra information on the right. 
[19:23.38]And now for the third step. 
[19:25.40]In class, a good note-taker should only write key points, not all the information. 
[19:32.34]Some students cannot understand what they wrote on notebook after class. 
[19:37.67]So how to master key points is very important. 
[19:41.18]These points are usually nouns, adjectives, verbs and numbers. 
[19:45.53]Writing only one to two points on each line can help you to understand the notes when you read them later.
[19:52.06]The most difficult part for nearly all students is, of course, to have enough time to write the notes.
[19:59.85]So the next step is coming. 
[20:02.03]In order to save time and speed up writing, I advise you to use abbreviations and symbols instead of words. 
[20:10.22]When your instructor speaks loudly or repeats some content or has a short pause, you should pay attention to the points related. 
[20:19.09]The last suggestion is that you should review your notes after class when the information is still fresh in your mind. 
[20:27.37]Try to summarize the key points and check notes with your classmates after class.
[20:33.49]I hope all these can help you with your note-taking in class.
[20:39.75]Questions 19 to 21 are based on the recording you have just heard.
[20:45.03]19. What factors can affect note-taking£¿
[21:02.66]20. What does the speaker say about a good note-taker£¿
[21:21.40]21. Why does the speaker suggest using abbreviations and symbols£¿
[21:41.88]Recording Three
[21:43.72]Good evening, ladies and gentlemen. 
[21:46.17]It's my honor to be here. 
[21:48.48]I am Roger Coffin, a nutritionist and researcher in food, and I have written many articles on food science, which is more and more important in society nowadays¡ªeven has a profound impact on our daily lives. 
[22:05.05]Today we are going to look at a food stuff: garlic. 
[22:09.32]We will explore a little of its history and examine some of the many different ways it's been used over time. 
[22:17.60]All around the world, for over 4,000 years, garlic has played many important roles in our daily lives. 
[22:26.97]Many countries grow garlic today, like China, Egypt, Spain, etc. 
[22:33.07]Garlic originates from areas where the summers are dry and hot. 
[22:37.83]In order to survive, the wild garlic plant had to adapt to this harsh climate. 
[22:44.00]Garlic had to grow when there was moisture, in the spring and fall, and it also had to be able to survive without water for the exceptionally dry summer and winter months. 
[22:56.10]Garlic adapted to its environment so that the growth cycle began in the fall, to take advantage of the available moisture. 
[23:05.49]When the cold winters or the hot dry summers arrived, it became inactive. 
[23:11.85]When the spring rains arrived, the plant could continue on its growth process. 
[23:17.40]A healthy root system and relatively small leaves is the key to the survival of the plant. 
[23:24.30]These traits make garlic a very healthy plant, capable of growing in poor soil in harsh climates with little or no care. 
[23:34.34]Once discovered by humans, it is no wonder that garlic quickly became an important crop of almost every civilization in the world.
[23:44.68]An Egyptian manuscript from 1500 BCE recommended garlic for 22 diseases. 
[23:52.87]The Egyptians fed it to slaves building the pyramids to increase their toughness. 
[23:58.71]Garlic was so valuable to the Egyptians that 15 pounds of garlic would purchase a healthy male slave. 
[24:06.83]In ancient Greece and Rome, garlic was claimed to have more uses¡ªeverything from repelling scorpions to treating dog bites. 
[24:17.14]In the Middle Ages, soldiers returning to Europe after faraway battles were generally credited with bringing garlic back with them, for it was thought to prevent the plague. 
[24:29.26]Garlic was so prized in ceremony and ritual, and it's said that lambs offered for sacrifice were seasoned with garlic to make them more pleasing to the gods. 
[24:41.93]OK. That's all for today's talk. Hope you all like it. Thank you!
